    "sharpest_arguments_from_750": [
      {
        "argument": "The Physical Inventory / Confiscation Paradox: 'Team A physically holds cards whose face values sum to +125. Capping them at 100 requires confiscating 25 points without textual authority.' (Heavily emphasized by 1, 6, 10, 11, 15, 18, 20)",
        "vulnerability_to_exploit": "Conflating card face values with scored trick points. In trick-taking games (e.g., Doppelkopf, Tarock), card points often sum to a nominal number, but the game payout/scoring metric caps or converts them. In Tichu, 'Es gibt im Spiel 100 Punkte zu verteilen' defines the total resource pool. Winning 125 positive card face value points simply means capturing 100% of the available positive pool, not 125%."
      },
      {
        "argument": "The Routine Negative Trick Total Refutation: Colleague 4 argued that if a team captures a 10 and the Phoenix, they score 10 - 25 = -15 in tricks without declaring, refuting the claim that trick scores cannot be negative without an announcement.",
        "vulnerability_to_exploit": "This is a dangerous tactical strike by Colleague 4 that threatens our core argument about negative scores. We must address this head-on: does a team actually record -15 on an official scoresheet if they made no announcement, or is that itself an artifact of unexamined casual play? Even if intermediate net trick negative totals occur when mixed with positive cards, a team holding ZERO positive cards cannot have an 'Abzug' applied because there is nothing to deduct from."
      },
      {
        "argument": "Bidirectional Symmetry: 100 - 125 = -25 and 100 - (-25) = 125 preserves the deck invariant (125 + (-25) = 100).",
        "vulnerability_to_exploit": "Hostettler's explicit qualifier 'In der Regel' directly proves this formula was never intended as an unconditional algebraic identity across all integers."
      }
    ]
  },
  "persuasion_targets_for_round_6": {
