        "colleague": 4,
        "profile": "Pragmatic rule-tester. Look at how he constructed the '10 + Phoenix = -15' test case. If we demonstrate that even in that case, the overall round framework treats card points within the bounded simplex of the 100-point deck economy, he can be swayed."
      },
      {
        "colleague": 1,
        "profile": "The intellectual anchor of Camp 750. He cares deeply about textual fidelity to Hostettler's German text. Focus relentlessly on Hostettler's specific terminology: 'Punkte zu verteilen' (points to distribute) vs 'Kartenwerte' (card face values)."
      },
      {
        "colleague": 11,
        "profile": "German game tradition scholar. He knows Swiss Jass. Reiterate how Swiss Jass handles penalty cards ('Strafkarten')—they penalize points made, but never award bonus surplus points to the opponent beyond the match total."
      }
    ]
  },
  "strategic_adjustments_for_round_6": {
    "arguments_to_deploy": [
      "1. Direct Counter to the 'Confiscation' Fallacy: Clarify the distinction between 'Kartenwert' (nominal card face value) and 'Stichpunkte' (distributable game points). Just as taking 13 tricks in Bridge or all tricks in Hearts doesn't award 'extra' points beyond the game's defined limit, capturing all positive cards captures 100% of the 100 distributable points. The +125 face value exists strictly inside the deck to absorb the Phoenix's -25, not to create a 125-point payout.",
      "2. The 'Doppelsieg' (Double Victory) Proportionality Argument: Colleague 7 introduced a brilliant, unrefuted ludological proof that must be magnified: Hostettler set the Double Victory at exactly 200 points because it is precisely double the maximum trick pot (100). If trick play could award 125 vs -25 (a 150-point net swing on a 1-3 finish), it completely breaks the economic scaling of the game, giving 75% of a double victory for a non-double victory!",
      "3. Dissecting the '10 + Phoenix = -15' Objection: Clarify that the Phoenix text states '-25 Punkte bei der Abrechnung'. In standard German card game jurisprudence, an 'Abrechnung' deduction offsets points won. But the distributable total of the game cannot exceed 100 points. If Team A gets 125, where did the extra 25 points come from? They were created out of thin air by turning Team B's penalty into Team A's asset.",
      "4. The Rio Grande / Fata Morgana English & German Rule Equivalence: In the Rio Grande English rules: 'There are 100 points in each hand.' It does not say 'There is a net balance of 100 points after subtracting liabilities.' It explicitly limits the hand's point content to 100."
    ],
    "rhetorical_stance": "Maintain absolute academic gravitas. Commend the algebraic elegance of 750, but frame it as an artificial programming model that violates tabletop game theory and Hostettler's explicit 100-point closed economic system."
  }
}

