        "vulnerability_to_exploit": "Digital game adaptations frequently streamline card-scoring logic (e.g. implementing scoring as `player.tricks.sum()` rather than hardcoding complex tabletop edge-case exception handling) for edge cases that occur once in ten thousand games. Video game implementations are not rulebook text. In published tabletop errata and tournament rules, rulebooks govern, not code. Hostettler collaborated on BSW's overall release, not line-by-line validation of theoretical boundary calculations."
      },
      {
        "argument": "The 'Ten + Phoenix = -15' Empirical Proof: If a team wins a Ten and the Phoenix without announcements, Camp 750 asserts they score -15 in actual play, proving trick play can be negative.",
        "vulnerability_to_exploit": "Camp 750 treats this as an established empirical fact, but in official tournament scorekeeping, an unannounced partnership cannot end a round with negative score points on the scoresheet. If a team has net negative tricks, tournament scoring software and paper sheets handle it either by setting trick points to 0 or treating it as an internal deduction against existing positive game score, but it NEVER creates an exportable +115 for the opponents out of a 100-point hand!"
      }
    ]
  },
